  "response": "## Answer: no, the repo ships no agent-facing context\n\n`crimes scan` flags it as `crime_00006` — **Missing Agent Context**, medium severity, confidence 0.90:\n\n```\nno AGENTS.md found at repo root\nno CLAUDE.md found at repo root\nno .claude/skills/*/SKILL.md present\nno .agents/skills/*/SKILL.md present\npackage.json declares bin(s): tsup, tsup-node — agents have no way to discover commands\n```\n\nI confirmed it directly against `evals/fixtures/04-monorepo`: the root has `README.md`, `CONTRIBUTING.md`, `docs/`, `.github/` — and none of the four agent surfaces. It's the only finding in the report about the repo *as a workspace*; the other 81 are about code.\n\nTwo notes on reading the output:\n\n- The finding is anchored to `assets/cjs_shims.js` only because repo-level findings need a file to hang on. It is not about that file.\n- This fixture is `tsup` at v8.5.1 pinned from upstream — despite the name, it's a single package, not a workspace monorepo. `coverage.by_package` listing `assets` and `test` is directory heuristics, not pnpm workspaces. There is no `pnpm-workspace.yaml`.\n\n## Why this matters for your first PR\n\nThe consequence isn't \"docs are missing\" — `CONTRIBUTING.md` and `docs/README.md` exist and are decent. It's that **nothing an agent loads by default tells it how this repo works**, so an agent starts each session inferring conventions from code it happens to open. Concretely, in this repo:\n\n- **`pnpm test` builds first.** It's `pnpm run build && pnpm run test-only`. An agent that reaches for `vitest run` gets a stale `dist/` and debugs a phantom failure — and most of the test suite (`test/utils.ts`) shells out to the built CLI, so a stale build produces failures that look like source bugs.\n- **Prettier is the formatter, enforced by a CI job that auto-commits.** Nothing in the tree says so except `.prettierrc` and a workflow file. An agent that hand-formats, or reaches for Biome/ESLint because it's a TS repo, fights a bot.\n- **The release path is `dev` → `main` merge, not tags.** An agent asked to \"cut a release\" has no way to know that without reading `CONTRIBUTING.md`, which it has no reason to open.\n- **Peer deps are optional by design** (`@swc/core`, `postcss`, `typescript`, `@microsoft/api-extractor`). An agent that \"fixes\" a resolution error by promoting one to a hard dependency breaks the package's install contract.\n\nThat last category is the expensive one. Agents fail loudly on missing information and quietly on *absent* information — they don't know there was a rule, so they make a confident, plausible, wrong edit. The other 81 findings compound this: `src/index.ts` has a 293-line `build()` and 17 fan-out, `src/utils.ts` has 18 importers. Those are exactly the files where an agent editing without context does the most damage.\n\n## Minimal AGENTS.md that clears the finding\n\nThe detector is satisfied by an `AGENTS.md` at root — it checks presence, not contents. So keep it minimal and *true*; a skeleton full of plausible-sounding invented rules is worse than nothing. Everything below is drawn from `package.json`, `CONTRIBUTING.md`, and `.github/workflows/`:\n\n```markdown\n# AGENTS.md\n\n`tsup` bundles TypeScript libraries with no config, powered by esbuild.\nSingle package — not a workspace. Node >= 18, pnpm.\n\n## Commands\n\n- `pnpm i` — install\n- `pnpm build` — bundle CLI + library entries to `dist/`\n- `pnpm build-fast` — build without .d.ts emit (faster inner loop)\n- `pnpm dev` — build-fast in watch mode\n- `pnpm test` — **builds, then runs vitest**. This is what CI runs.\n- `pnpm test-only` — vitest only; requires a current `dist/`\n- `pnpm format` — Prettier over the repo\n\n## Before you edit\n\nMost tests in `test/` execute the built CLI, not the source. If you change\n`src/` and run `pnpm test-only`, you are testing the previous build. Use\n`pnpm test`, or rebuild first.\n\n## Conventions\n\n- Prettier is the only formatter (`.prettierrc`). A CI job reformats and\n  pushes; do not hand-format or add a second formatter.\n- `@microsoft/api-extractor`, `@swc/core`, `postcss`, and `typescript` are\n  *optional* peer dependencies. Do not promote them to dependencies to\n  resolve a local error — guard the import instead.\n- Add tests under `test/`.\n\n## Layout\n\n- `src/index.ts` — main build orchestration (large; read before editing)\n- `src/cli-main.ts` — CLI entry, shared by `cli-default.ts` / `cli-node.ts`\n- `src/esbuild/` — esbuild pipeline and plugins\n- `src/rollup.ts`, `src/rollup/` — .d.ts rollup path\n- `src/utils.ts` — widely imported (18 importers); changes here reach far\n\n## Releasing\n\nMerge PRs into `dev`, then `dev` → `main`. Pushing `main` triggers the\nGitHub Action that publishes to npm. Do not publish by hand.\n```\n\nThat's ~40 lines and clears `crime_00006` at its stated `effort: small`.\n\n**I haven't written the file.** This is a pinned eval fixture (`c5e9cd9` of `egoist/tsup`), and adding `AGENTS.md` would change the scan baseline for every future eval run against `04-monorepo`. If you want it landed somewhere, say where — in a real clone of tsup it's a clean first-PR contribution on its own.\n\nOne product note while I'm here: this finding's `fix_shape` says *\"write a SKILL.md so agents discover the entry points\"* while its `suggested_actions[0]` says *\"Add AGENTS.md, a Claude skill, or a Codex skill.\"* The narrow one is the headline and the accurate one is buried. AGENTS.md is the lower-effort, more portable answer for most repos — the `fix_shape` should probably lead with it.",
